            "**3** Critics argue that a later start would simply push everything back: sport, part-time jobs and family routines would all suffer, and teenagers would stay up later still. These are fair concerns, and any change would need careful planning. Yet schools overseas that have moved their start time by as little as 45 minutes have reported fewer late arrivals and better attendance, and students there say they feel more alert in morning classes.\n\n"
            "**4** Nobody is suggesting that a later bell will solve every problem teenagers face. It is, however, one of the few changes that costs almost nothing and works with the body rather than against it. We would never schedule a toddler's nap at midday and then wonder why it went badly. Why do we expect teenagers to learn algebra at a time when their brains are still, in effect, asleep?"
